    {
      "filename": "The Maiden, National Museum of Scotland.jpg",
      "title": "File:The Maiden, National Museum of Scotland.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/The_Maiden%2C_National_Museum_of_Scotland.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Maiden,_National_Museum_of_Scotland.jpg",
      "width": 1920,
      "height": 2560,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0",
      "artist": "Kim Traynor",
      "credit": "Own work",
      "source": "The Maiden, National Museum of Scotland",
      "description": "\"The Scottish machine is made of oak and consists of a sole beam 5 feet in length into which are fixed two upright posts 10 feet in height, 4 inches broad and 12 inches apart from each other, and 3 1/2 inches in thickness, with bevelled corners. These posts are kept steady by a brace at each side which springs from the end of the sole and is fastened to the uprights 4 feet from the bottom. The tops of the posts are fixed into a cross rail 2 feet in length. The block is a transverse bar 3 1/4 feet from the bottom, 8 inches in breadth and 4 1/2 inches in thickness, and a hollow on the upper edge of this bar is filled with lead... The axe consists of a plate of iron faced with steel; it measures 13 inches in length and 10 1/2 inches in breadth. On the upper edge of the plate was fixed a mass of lead 75 lbs in weight. This blade works in grooves cut on the inner edges of the uprights, which are lined with copper...\" -- Proceedings of the Society of Antiquities of Scotland, Vol.III, 1886-8",
      "dateCreated": "2011-07-25",
      "sourceType": "core",
      "sourceArticleId": "national-museum-of-scotland-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gcvw",
